The connection between the Fire Alarm Control Panel and the Annunciator shown in the diagram represents the communication interface with the fire department. Additionally, the system diagram includes various types of Schedules, which may differ in presentation format.

Therefore, strictly speaking, this is not merely a Zone Schedule, but rather an integration of the Zone Schedule with Sequence Operations. A Zone Schedule is typically used to list the zones to which devices belong and their corresponding parameters, whereas this design combines it with linkage logic.

Specifically, upon fire confirmation (fire signals may come from smoke detectors, heat detectors, multi-sensor detectors, water flow switches, pressure switches, etc.), the system will execute the following linkage operations:

Immediately activate building-wide audible and visual alarms (including alarms and speakers);

Simultaneously send a signal to the monitoring center, which will relay it to the fire department;

Immediately activate building-wide audible and visual alarms (including alarms and speakers);

Simultaneously send a signal to the monitoring center, which will relay it to the fire department;

Shut down the HVAC system. It should be noted that the HVAC shutdown strategy is divided into three types (not elaborated here).

It is important to emphasize that the above operations are not executed sequentially but are triggered simultaneously. The Fire Alarm Sequence Operation fully defines the system response process upon fire confirmation.

One approach is to directly shut down the corresponding air handling unit (AHU) via hardwired interlock when a duct smoke detector detects a fire signal, but this method is not recommended.
